# What must NOT reach the Docker build context.
#
# `docker/web.Dockerfile` does `COPY . .` after copying an already-installed
# node_modules from its `deps` stage. Without this file three things go wrong:
#
#   1. `.env` is copied into the image. The API receives its configuration at
#      RUNTIME through compose's `env_file`, so a credential in the image is
#      pure downside: it survives in the layer and can be read back out of the
#      image by anyone who has it.
#   2. The host's node_modules overwrites the one installed inside the image.
#      Those were built for the host's platform, so an image built on an Apple
#      Silicon Mac for linux/amd64 ends up with the wrong native binaries.
#   3. The whole repository — node_modules, .git, the Python virtualenv — is
#      uploaded to the daemon on every build.
#
# Anything the build genuinely needs must stay OUT of this list: the workspace
# manifests, apps/web, apps/api/app, packages/, and scripts/, which the web
# build runs through its `prebuild` step to copy the PDF.js assets.

# Credentials. Never in an image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Installed dependencies: both images install their own.
node_modules
**/node_modules
.venv
**/.venv
venv

# Build output: the image builds its own.
.next
**/.next
out
**/out
*.tsbuildinfo

# Version control and CI.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Python build and cache artefacts.
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info
**/*.egg-info
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache

# Test output and generated fixtures.
test-results
**/test-results
playwright-report
**/playwright-report
coverage
**/coverage
**/tests/e2e/.generated

# The API's runtime temporary directory. In compose this is a tmpfs mount.
apps/api/tmp

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
